WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:03.320
Let’s say you’re trying to
find some solid information

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:05.780
about a serious health
problem that concerns you,

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:08.440
like high blood pressure,
or diabetes.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.080
Yet, everywhere you look,

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.800
someone’s trying to sell you
something like vitamins,

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:17.080
yoga mats, blenders, drugs.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.680
Well, breathe a
sigh of relief

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.800
because all we bring
you are the facts.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.200
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.520
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.560
Did you know that there
has been a steep rise

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:31.720
in cases of asthma
over the years

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:33.800
and that much of that
can be attributed

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.360
to an increase in
air pollution?

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.720
Well, it turns out that some diets
can improve our respiratory defense

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:42.680
against lung disease
and infection.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:45.640
Here’s our first story.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:51.120
Outdoor air pollution may be the 9th
leading cause of death and disability

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:55.560
in the world, responsible for millions
of deaths from lung cancer,

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:59.480
emphysema, heart disease,
stroke, and respiratory infection.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:03.000
In the U.S., living in a polluted
city was associated

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:09.880
with a 16, 27, and 28% increase in total,
cardiovascular, and lung cancer death,

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:13.400
compared to living in
a city with cleaner air.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:17.560
Living in a city with polluted air
may lead to up to a 75% increase

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:20.080
in the risk of a heart attack.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:27.320
No one wants to be living in a traffic jam,
but it's better than dying in a traffic jam.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.240
In addition to causing deaths,

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:32.240
air pollution is also the cause of
a number of health problems.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:35.120
It may not only
exacerbate asthma,

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:39.320
but also increase the risk of
developing asthma in the first place.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:43.360
These pollutants may
trigger liver disease,

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.720
even increase the
risk of diabetes.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:51.640
Even when atmospheric pollutants
are within legally established limits,

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:56.160
they can be harmful to health.
So what can we do about it?

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:00.360
Paper after paper describing all the
terrible things air pollution can do to us,

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.960
but most failed to
mention public policy.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:07.360
We're making great strides in
demonstrating the harmful effects,

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:12.360
but public authorities are not using
these data to reduce emissions,

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:15.280
as they might
inconvenience the population

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:19.560
and therefore might not be
politically acceptable.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:24.480
To treat the cause, we need
better vehicle inspections,

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.880
efficient public transport,
bus lanes, bicycle lanes,

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:31.520
even urban tolls to
help clean up the air.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:33.000
While we're waiting for all that,

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:37.040
is there anything we can
do to protect ourselves?

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:41.480
Well, our body naturally
has detoxifying enzymes,

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:45.360
not only in our liver
but lining our airways.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:49.960
Studies showing that people born
with less effective detox enzymes

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:53.960
have an exaggerated allergic
response to diesel exhaust,

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:57.760
suggesting that these enzymes
actively combat the inflammation

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.160
caused by pollutants in the air.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:04.680
A significant part of the population has
these substandard forms of the enzyme,

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.920
but either way, what can
we do to boost the activity

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:11.120
of whichever detoxification
enzymes we do have?

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:15.880
Well, if you remember, broccoli
can dramatically boost the activity

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:21.120
of the detox enzymes in our liver,
but what about our lungs?

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:25.600
Researchers fed some smokers a large
stalk of broccoli every day for 10 days

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:29.840
to see if it would affect the level
of inflammation within their bodies.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:32.400
Why smokers?
Because smoking

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.480
is so inflammatory
that you can end up

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:41.720
with elevated C-reactive protein levels
for up to 30 years after quitting,

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:46.080
and that inflammation can start almost
immediately after we start smoking,

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:51.400
so it's critical to never
start in the first place.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:56.360
But if you do, you can cut your level
of that inflammation biomarker, CRP,

00:03:56.360 --> 00:04:02.360
nearly in half, after just 10 days
eating a lot of broccoli.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:05.800
Appears to cut inflammation
in nonsmokers as well,

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:09.640
maybe explaining in part why eating
more than two cups of broccoli,

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:12.640
cabbage, cauliflower, kale, or
other cruciferous veggies a day

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:17.160
is associated with 20%
reduced risk of dying,

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:21.240
compared to eating a third
of a cup a day or less.

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:23.720
So what about air pollution?

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:27.320
We know the cruciferous compound
is the most potent known inducer

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.360
of our detox enzymes.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:34.531
And so most of the research has
been its ability to fight cancer,

00:04:34.531 --> 00:04:36.520
but here for the first time, they
tried to see if it could combat

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:41.280
the pro-inflammatory impact of
pollutants such as diesel exhaust.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:44.320
They put some human lung
lining cells in a Petri dish

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:44.840
and this is how many detox
enzymes are produced.

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:47.720
Drip on some broccoli goodness
and you get this many.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:51.760
Yeah, but we don't inhale broccoli,
we don't snort it; we eat it.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:54.720
Can it still get into
our lungs and help?

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.840
Yes, two days of broccoli
sprout consumption,

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.200
then you suck some
cells out of their nose,

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:03.880
and up to 100 times more
detox enzyme expression

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:07.680
compared to eating a non-cruciferous
vegetable, alfalfa sprouts.

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:12.720
Now, all we have to do is squirt
some diesel exhaust up their nose,

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:16.360
which is what some
UCLA researchers did,

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:22.400
an amount equal to daily rush hour
exposure on a Los Angeles freeway.

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:25.800
Within six hours, the number of
inflammatory cells in their nose

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:29.800
shot up and continued to rise.

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:33.320
But in the group that had been
getting a broccoli sprout extract,

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:39.200
the inflammation went
down and stayed down.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:41.280
Since the dose in these
studies is equivalent

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:44.000
to the consumption of
1 or 2 cups of broccoli,

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:47.040
their study demonstrates
the potential preventive

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:50.320
and therapeutic
potential of broccoli.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:53.400
But if broccoli is so powerful at
suppressing this inflammatory

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:58.360
immune response, might it interfere
with normal immune function?

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:03.280
After all the battle with viruses like
influenza can happen in the nose.

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:05.120
Let's drip some flu viruses

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:10.360
into the nostrils of broccoli
sprout eaters and find out.

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:13.600
And what you get is
the best of both worlds:

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:17.763
less inflammation, yet an
improved immune response.

00:06:17.836 --> 00:06:21.080
After eating a package of
broccoli sprouts every day,

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:25.040
our body is able to keep
the virus in check,

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:28.080
potentially offering a safe,
low-cost strategy

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:33.000
for reducing influenza risk
among high risk populations.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:37.080
So better immune function,
yet less inflammation,

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:40.600
potentially reducing the impact
of pollution on allergic disease

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:45.880
and asthmaâ€”at least for an
enthusiastic broccoli consumer.

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:47.720
But what about cancer,

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:51.120
detoxifying air pollutants
throughout the rest of our body?

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:55.320
We didn't know until now.

00:06:55.320 --> 00:07:00.280
Off to China where they have some
of the worst air pollution in the world.

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:03.160
And by day one, those
getting the broccoli sprouts

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:07.040
were able to get rid of 60%
more benzene from their bodies,

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:11.680
a rapid, highly durable elevation
in the detoxification

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:14.840
of a known human carcinogen.

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:18.120
Now this was using broccoli sprouts,
which are highly concentrated,

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:21.360
equivalent to about 5 cups
of broccoli a day.

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:25.200
So we don't know how well
more modest doses would work,

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:29.400
but if they do, eating broccoli
could provide a frugal means

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:34.560
to attenuate the long-term
health risks of air pollution.

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:35.480
Finally today,

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:41.080
if the nitrites in foods like ham
and bacon cause lung damage,

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:45.440
what about processed meat
with “no nitrites added”?

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:49.560
Recently, the World Health Organization
classified processed meat,

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:54.240
also known as cured meat—bacon, ham,
hot dogs, lunch meat, and sausage—

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:59.560
as definitively cancer-
causing in humans.

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:02.640
As if that’s not enough, high
processed meat consumption

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:06.360
has also been associated with
increased risk of dying prematurely

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:09.560
from all causes put together
and is a risk factor

00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:14.360
for several major chronic diseases,
such as type 2 diabetes,

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:17.080
coronary heart disease, and stroke.

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:21.200
But what about lung issues like asthma?

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:24.760
Nitrites are added to processed
meats as preservatives

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:28.360
to preserve their pink
(so they don’t turn gray),

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:30.800
keeping them less rancid
tasting, and to prevent

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:34.120
the growth of diseases like botulism.

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:37.240
But put that same sodium
nitrite in the drinking water

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:40.280
of lab animals and
they develop emphysema.

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:44.240
They nearly all developed emphysema.

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:46.782
But that’s all the scientific
knowledge we had on the subject

00:08:46.782 --> 00:08:49.840
coming into 2007, until this
study, which found that

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:53.240
frequent cured meat consumption
is associated with increased risk

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:57.800
for developing diseases like
emphysema in people, too–

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:00.280
chronic obstructive
pulmonary disease (COPD).

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:04.240
Eating it like every other
day appeared to triple the odds

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:06.480
of severe COPD.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:09.280
But it was just a snapshot-in-time
study; so, we don’t know

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:12.520
which came first, the
sausage or the COPD.

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:16.880
For that, we need prospective studies
that follow people over time,

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:21.520
and the big twin Harvard
studies in both women and men

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:24.720
both found that the risk
of newly diagnosed COPD

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:29.240
increased with a greater
consumption of cured meats.

00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:32.960
Currently, we now have studies involving
hundreds of thousands of people,

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:36.320
showing that higher intakes of
processed meat were associated with

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:39.920
a 40% increased risk of COPD.

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:42.520
It comes out to like an 8% higher risk

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:47.120
of COPD for each hot
dog you eat all week

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:53.240
or each weekly breakfast link
sausage. What is going on?

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:57.040
Yes, there are advanced glycation
end-products, so-called glycotoxins,

00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:01.120
that may be proinflammatory;
there’s the saturated fat

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:04.880
that can also trigger
inﬂammation in the airways;

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:09.360
the high salt content can present a
potential risk for lung inflammation;

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:14.560
or the increase in systemic
inflammation in general.

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:17.160
But the reason attention
has focused on the nitrites

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:20.480
is because nitrites may actually
be one of the mechanisms

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:24.880
by which tobacco smoke causes
diseases like emphysema.

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:29.160
Yes, cured meats are the principal
source of dietary nitrites,

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:32.240
but nitrites are also
byproducts of tobacco smoke.

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:36.520
One of the main constituents, besides
the carbon monoxide and nicotine,

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:41.800
are nitrogen oxides that are
converted in the lung to nitrites.

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:45.440
The way nitrites appear to
cause lung damage is by affecting

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:49.760
connective tissue proteins
like collagen and elastin.

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:53.560
That’s what helps keep the
airspaces in your lungs open,

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:57.720
but nitrite can modify these
proteins in a way that mimic

00:10:57.720 --> 00:11:03.440
age-related damage, including
the fragmentation of elastin.

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:07.000
With that much lung injury,
it’s logical to assume

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:11.320
processed meat consumption could
also exacerbate the disease

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:13.240
of those who already have it.

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:18.240
And indeed, cured meat consumption
increases risk of COPD patients

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:23.560
ending up back in the hospital, about
twice the risk for those eating

00:11:23.560 --> 00:11:28.960
more than average, and it appears
the more you eat, the worse it is.

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:33.400
Regarding lung health, processed meat
intake has been associated with

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:37.760
a likely increased risk of lung cancer,
a decline in lung function,

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:40.600
and chronic obstructive
pulmonary diseases.

00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:43.160
But, what about asthma?

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:45.400
High processed meat consumption
has been associated with

00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:48.000
higher asthma symptoms as well.

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:51.840
We knew about the higher maternal
intake of meat before pregnancy,

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:55.920
potentially increasing the risk of
wheezing in her children later on

00:11:55.920 --> 00:12:00.880
(based on a study of more than
a thousand mother-child pairs).

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:04.480
And we’re not talking about
aspirating meat into your lungs

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:07.520
and being misdiagnosed with asthma.

00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:11.080
Those who ate the most cured meats
were 76% more likely

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:15.720
to experience worsening asthma
than those who ate the least.

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:19.680
Since obesity is a likely
risk factor for asthma, though,

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:22.480
maybe the influence of the
meat is just indirect,

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:27.200
by contributing to weight gain?
That might be a small part of it,

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:30.240
but the main effect appears to be direct,

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:34.640
suggesting a deleterious role of
cured meat independent of weight.

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:38.560
Put all those studies together and
processed meat intake appears to be

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:44.400
an important target for the prevention
of adult asthma in the first place.

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:47.520
Even if you don’t have any lung
issues, processed meat consumption

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:51.800
was negatively associated with
measures of normal lung function,

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:55.640
while fruit and vegetable consumption
and dietary total antioxidant

00:12:55.640 --> 00:12:58.840
capacity was associated
with better lung function.

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:05.400
But wait, you say. I just eat
all natural, uncured hot dogs,

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:10.160
with NO NITRATES OR NITRITES
ADDED, in all caps.

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:14.480
But if you magnifying glass
the small print, it says

00:13:14.480 --> 00:13:17.760
“except those naturally occurring in…
cultured celery juice.”

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:22.360
See, to avoid saying they added
nitrites, what they do is

00:13:22.360 --> 00:13:26.200
add something that has a lot of
nitrates, like celery and a bacteria

00:13:26.200 --> 00:13:28.400
that converts the nitrates to nitrites.

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:31.200
So, they are adding nitrites.

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:34.920
They’re just straight-up duping consumers.

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:38.720
We didn’t add any nitrites
except, of course…

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:41.080
for all the nitrites we added.

00:13:41.080 --> 00:13:45.080
We care about your health;
so, no nitrites added.

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:47.880
Who wants pepperoni with nitrites?

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:51.760
So, we just added lots of nitrites.

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:55.200
We would never add any nitrites.

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:58.240
Now, just let the piggy picture
distract you from the fact

00:13:58.240 --> 00:14:05.040
that we just lied to your face.
Hormel was my favorite.

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:09.880
“Except for those naturally occurring
in seasoning”—pretty slick.

00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:12.920
Europe doesn’t allow this
kind of consumer fraud,

00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:17.520
demanding manufacturers explicitly
label it as containing nitrites.

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:20.040
You can’t even call it natural.

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:24.120
When Consumer Reports put it to the
test, they found the nitrite levels

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:26.960
in all the products were
essentially the same;

00:14:26.960 --> 00:14:31.840
so “no nitrites” doesn’t mean no nitrites.

00:14:31.840 --> 00:14:35.120
Consumer Reports and the Center
for Science in the Public Interest

00:14:35.120 --> 00:14:39.960
have petitioned to stop
this misleading practice.

00:14:39.960 --> 00:14:41.520
Nitrites are nitrites.

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:43.920
“Their chemical composition
is absolutely the same,

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:47.640
and so are the health eﬀects.”

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:49.880
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:14:49.880 --> 00:14:53.040
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:55.560
Go to nutritionfacts.org/testimonials.

00:14:55.560 --> 00:14:58.680
We may share it on our social
media to help inspire others.

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:05.280
To see any graphs, charts, graphics,
images, or studies mentioned here,

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:07.840
go to the Nutrition Facts
Podcast landing page.

00:15:07.840 --> 00:15:10.220
There you’ll find all the
detailed information you need –

00:15:10.220 --> 00:15:14.880
plus, links to all of the sources
we cite for each of these topics.

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:17.600
My last two books were
“How to Survive a Pandemic”

00:15:17.600 --> 00:15:20.400
and my “How Not to Diet Cookbook”.

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:23.480
Get ready this year for the
launch of “How Not to Age”.

00:15:23.480 --> 00:15:25.480
And, of course, all the proceeds
I receive from the sales

00:15:25.480 --> 00:15:28.640
